.\" clipt(1) -- screenshot and screen recording tool for Wayland and X11.
.TH CLIPT 1 "2026-10-31" "clipt" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
clipt \- screenshot and screen recording tool for Wayland and X11
.SH SYNOPSIS
.na
.B clipt
[\fIoptions\fR] [\fIcommand\fR] [\fIargument\fR ...]
.br
.B clipt record
[\fBscreen\fR [\fIname\fR] | \fBwindow\fR [\fIapp\fR] |
\fB\-g\fR \fIgeometry\fR | \fB\-\-stop\fR | \fB\-\-pause\fR |
\fB\-\-resume\fR | \fB\-\-toggle\-pause\fR]
.ad
.SH DESCRIPTION
\fBclipt\fR freezes the screen, lets you drag a region, annotate it, and
then copy or save the result.
It is one small binary with no GUI toolkit: the overlay is drawn by clipt
itself, and captures use the compositor's own protocols.
.PP
On Wayland the capture backend is chosen automatically: ext-image-copy-capture
or wlr-screencopy on wlroots compositors, KWin's ScreenShot2 interface on KDE
Plasma, and xdg-desktop-portal on GNOME.
On X11 it uses GetImage (RandR/Xinerama monitors) and supports INCR, so
large images paste into any application.
Every output gets its own overlay at its native resolution, so mixed
resolutions, fractional scaling and rotated monitors work.
.PP
\fBclipt record\fR records an output, a region or a window to MP4.
On wlroots compositors it uses the capture protocols directly; on KDE Plasma
and GNOME it goes through KWin's and Mutter's screencast interfaces over
PipeWire.
Recording needs \fBffmpeg\fR; X11 recording is planned but not supported yet.
.PP
After copying, clipt forks into the background to serve the clipboard until
something else is copied, because a Wayland clipboard is owned by a live
client.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B gui
Select a region and annotate it.
This is the default when no \fIcommand\fR is given.
The overlay opens on every output; drag a selection on any of them.
.TP
.B full
Capture the whole desktop, stitching every output into one image.
.TP
\fBscreen\fR [\fIname\fR]
Capture one output, named as shown by \fBlist\fR; the first output is used
when \fIname\fR is omitted.
.TP
.B list
Print each output's name, size, position and description, then the capture
backends available on this desktop.
.TP
.B record
Open the selection overlay in record mode: drag an area, or click a window
or screen, then press \fBEnter\fR to start.
A red frame outside the recorded area marks it; the frame is not in the
video.
The recording is saved in \fBvideo_dir\fR by default.
\fBrecord region\fR is accepted as a synonym for plain \fBrecord\fR.
.TP
\fBrecord screen\fR [\fIname\fR]
Record one output without opening the overlay, named as shown by
\fBlist\fR; the first output is used when \fIname\fR is omitted.
.TP
\fBrecord window\fR [\fIapp\fR]
Record the topmost window whose app ID is \fIapp\fR.
With no \fIapp\fR, the overlay opens so that a window can be picked; a
picked window is followed as it moves and resizes.
.TP
\fBrecord\fR \fB\-g\fR \fIgeometry\fR
Record the region \fIgeometry\fR, in slurp's "X,Y WxH" format and logical
pixels, without opening the overlay.
The region must lie within one output.
.TP
\fBrecord\fR \fB\-\-stop\fR
Stop the running recording and wait for the file to be finished.
.TP
\fBrecord\fR \fB\-\-pause\fR
Pause a running recording.
Paused time is left out of the video and the frame turns amber.
.TP
\fBrecord\fR \fB\-\-resume\fR
Resume a paused recording.
.TP
\fBrecord\fR \fB\-\-toggle\-pause\fR
Pause a running recording, or resume it if it is already paused.
.TP
.B tray
Sit in the system tray (StatusNotifierItem).
While idle, left-click captures a region and middle-click copies the whole
desktop; while recording, clicking the icon stops the recording.
The right-click menu has a 3-second-delay capture, recording controls, the
screenshots and recordings folders, an update check, and Quit.
Each capture runs as a fresh clipt process.
.TP
\fBupdate\fR [\fB\-\-check\fR]
Check GitHub for a newer release and, for installs that clipt manages,
download it and replace the binary.
Packaged installs are pointed at their package manager.
With \fB\-\-check\fR, only report whether an update is available.
Needs \fBcurl\fR.
.SH OPTIONS
Options may appear before or after the command.
.TP
\fB\-o\fR, \fB\-\-output\fR \fIPATH\fR
Write the PNG to \fIPATH\fR instead of the save directory; \fB\-\fR writes
it to standard output.
With \fBrecord\fR, \fIPATH\fR is the video file and must end in \fB.mp4\fR,
\fB.mkv\fR or \fB.mov\fR.
.TP
\fB\-c\fR, \fB\-\-clipboard\fR
Copy the capture to the clipboard.
Applies to \fBfull\fR and \fBscreen\fR.
.TP
\fB\-g\fR, \fB\-\-geometry\fR \fIGEOM\fR
Crop a \fBfull\fR or \fBscreen\fR capture to \fIGEOM\fR in logical pixels,
in slurp's "X,Y WxH" format.
With \fBrecord\fR, records that region.
.TP
\fB\-d\fR, \fB\-\-delay\fR \fISECS\fR
Wait \fISECS\fR seconds before capturing or recording.
Fractional seconds are accepted.
.TP
\fB\-b\fR, \fB\-\-backend\fR \fINAME\fR
Force a capture backend: \fBext\fR, \fBwlr\fR, \fBkwin\fR, \fBportal\fR or
\fBx11\fR.
.TP
\fB\-\-cursor\fR
Include the mouse pointer in the capture, where the backend supports it.
.TP
\fB\-\-fps\fR \fIN\fR
Recording frame rate, 1 to 240 (default 30); overrides \fBrecord_fps\fR.
.TP
\fB\-\-stop\fR, \fB\-\-pause\fR, \fB\-\-resume\fR, \fB\-\-toggle\-pause\fR
Control a running recording; see \fBrecord\fR under COMMANDS.
.TP
\fB\-\-check\fR
With \fBupdate\fR, only check for a newer release; do not install it.
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Show help and exit.
.TP
\fB\-V\fR, \fB\-\-version\fR
Show the version and exit.
.SH EDITOR KEYS
.TP
.B drag
Select a region.
A click selects the highlighted window or the whole screen; drag the
handles to resize the selection and drag inside it to move it.
The window or screen under the pointer is highlighted before you click;
\fBTab\fR switches between a window and its screen.
.TP
\fBV\fR, \fBR\fR, \fBE\fR, \fBA\fR, \fBL\fR, \fBP\fR, \fBM\fR, \fBT\fR, \fBX\fR, \fBN\fR
Select, rectangle, ellipse, arrow, line, pen, highlighter, text, pixelate
and numbered counter.
.TP
\fB1\fR\-\fB8\fR
Colour: red, orange, yellow, green, blue, purple, white, black.
.TP
\fBscroll\fR, \fB[\fR, \fB]\fR
Stroke width.
.TP
.B Shift
Constrain to squares and 45\(de lines.
.TP
.B arrows
Nudge the selection; hold \fBShift\fR to resize it.
.TP
\fBEnter\fR, \fBCtrl+C\fR, double-click
Copy the annotated image and exit.
.TP
.B Ctrl+S
Save to \fBsave_dir\fR, and copy too when \fBcopy_on_save\fR is set.
.TP
\fBCtrl+Z\fR, \fBCtrl+Shift+Z\fR
Undo and redo.
.TP
\fBEsc\fR, right-click
Cancel with \fBEsc\fR; right-click backs out a step.
.SH CONFIGURATION
Settings live in \fIconfig.toml\fR under \fI$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/clipt\fR
(\fI~/.config/clipt\fR when \fBXDG_CONFIG_HOME\fR is unset).
The file is TOML and every key is optional; unknown keys are an error.
A leading \fB~/\fR in a path is expanded to the home directory.
.PP
The following keys are understood.
.TP
\fBsave_dir\fR = \fI"~/Pictures/Screenshots"\fR
Where \fBCtrl+S\fR, \fBfull\fR and \fBscreen\fR save screenshots.
.TP
\fBfilename\fR = \fI"Screenshot_%Y-%m-%d_%H-%M-%S"\fR
strftime pattern for new screenshots, without the extension.
.TP
\fBcolor\fR = \fI1\fR
Starting colour, 1 to 8: red, orange, yellow, green, blue, purple, white,
black.
.TP
\fBwidth\fR = \fI4\fR
Starting stroke width in logical pixels.
.TP
\fBcopy_on_save\fR = \fItrue\fR
Saving from the editor also copies the image to the clipboard.
.TP
\fBnotify\fR = \fItrue\fR
Show a desktop notification after saving or copying.
.TP
\fBbackend\fR = \fI"auto"\fR
Capture backend: \fBauto\fR, \fBext\fR, \fBwlr\fR, \fBkwin\fR,
\fBportal\fR or \fBx11\fR.
.TP
\fBcursor\fR = \fIfalse\fR
Include the mouse pointer in captures, where the backend supports it.
.TP
.B font
Path to a TTF or OTF file for editor text.
When unset (the default), fontconfig's sans-serif font is used.
.TP
\fBcheck_updates\fR = \fIfalse\fR
\fBclipt tray\fR checks GitHub once a day for a newer release.
.TP
\fBvideo_dir\fR = \fI"~/Videos/Screencasts"\fR
Where recordings are saved.
.TP
\fBvideo_filename\fR = \fI"Recording_%Y-%m-%d_%H-%M-%S"\fR
strftime pattern for new recordings, without the extension.
.TP
\fBrecord_fps\fR = \fI30\fR
Recording frame rate; \fB\-\-fps\fR overrides it.
.TP
\fBrecord_codec\fR = \fI"auto"\fR
Encoder: \fBauto\fR, \fBvaapi\fR, \fBnvenc\fR or \fBx264\fR.
\fBauto\fR uses the first one that works.
.TP
\fBmax_duration\fR = \fI3600\fR
A recording stops by itself after this many seconds; 0 means no limit.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B CLIPT_DEBUG
When set (for example \fBCLIPT_DEBUG=1\fR), clipt prints startup timings
and extra diagnostics to standard error.
Bug reports are most useful with this output.
.SH FILES
.TP
\fI$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/clipt/config.toml\fR
Configuration file.
Defaults to \fI~/.config/clipt/config.toml\fR when \fBXDG_CONFIG_HOME\fR is
unset.
.TP
\fI~/Pictures/Screenshots\fR
Default \fBsave_dir\fR for screenshots.
.TP
\fI~/Videos/Screencasts\fR
Default \fBvideo_dir\fR for recordings.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success, including a cancelled selection and \fB\-\-help\fR or
\fB\-\-version\fR.
.TP
.B 1
An error occurred; clipt prints a message to standard error.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR ffmpeg (1),
.BR slurp (1)
.PP
\fIhttps://github.com/jasondyck/clipt\fR
